  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Ledger",
    "overview": "intermediary is a record. It keeps what you meant to do next to what actually happened, and the screens should read like a careful ledger: neutral paper, one clear mark for what is live, and no decoration that competes with the entries. The product is a single person's working tool, so the system favors legibility over atmosphere. Density is moderate. Lists, boards, and forms stay scannable at a glance, and the week is the largest object on the page.\n\nThe surfaces are flat. Cards and columns are separated by hairline rules and a very light shadow, not by gradients or glow. Color is rationed: the signal blue marks what is active or actionable, and the status palette is the only other color on a screen. Everything is light-mode only; the page declares color-scheme: light.\n\nType is the system UI stack, sized small and set in Tailwind's default scale. Hierarchy comes from weight, size, and gray value, not from a display face.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat surfaces with hairline borders; shadow only on overlays and drag state.",
      "Neutral gray scale with one accent (signal blue) and a status palette.",
      "Dense but legible; the week and the status board are the primary objects.",
      "System UI typography; no custom font loading.",
      "Light mode only."
    ],
    "rules": [
      { "name": "The One Signal Rule", "body": "Signal blue marks the one thing the user can act on right now. A screen with several blue calls to action has lost its order.", "section": "colors" },
      { "name": "The Honest Status Rule", "body": "A status carries the same tone on every screen. Never assign a status a second color because a layout looks better that way.", "section": "colors" },
      { "name": "The Tabular Numbers Rule", "body": "Any figure that a user compares across tiles or rows uses tabular numerals.", "section": "typography" },
      { "name": "The Hairline Rule", "body": "Surfaces are separated by a one-pixel gray-200 border before they are separated by a shadow. A shadow is added only to show that something is floating above the page.", "section": "elevation" }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use one Signal Blue primary action per dialog or panel.",
      "Do keep status tones consistent with Pill.tsx. Adding a status means adding its tone there.",
      "Do separate surfaces with a one-pixel border first and a shadow second.",
      "Do use tabular numerals for counts, durations, and dates that are compared.",
      "Do show overdue state in red-600 text, not with a red fill on the whole card.",
      "Do leave focus to the base Focus Blue outline unless the element has its own ring, and inset it where a container would clip it."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add a second accent color for branding. The accent is the signal blue.",
      "Don't use gradients, glow, or frosted panels. The sticky header's 90% white blur is the only translucency in the system.",
      "Don't add dark mode styles without a decision to support it. The page declares light only.",
      "Don't give a resting card anything heavier than shadow-xs, or stack more than one shadow on an element.",
      "Don't remove focus rings from interactive elements.",
      "Don't introduce a custom display typeface. The system stack is the product's voice."
    ]
  }
